106 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (Calculating the result of a student manually requires adding the marks, finding the percentage and deciding the grade from the grade scale. Such manual work can lead to calculation mistakes. A program that performs these steps automatically makes the result quick and reliable.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
107 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (The system stores the marks of the five subjects in an array in subject order. The total, percentage and grade are calculated using loops, and the highest and lowest scoring subjects are identified. The marks can be sorted from high to low, and a particular mark can be searched using Linear Search.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
108 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (File handling is implemented to save the student record in a text file named marks.txt. The previously saved record is automatically loaded when the application starts, ensuring that the marks remain available between executions.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
109 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (This project demonstrates a simple Marks & Grade Calculator developed using Java. Users interact with the application through a menu-driven console interface to enter marks, view the result, find the highest and lowest scoring subjects, sort the marks and search for a particular mark.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
110 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (The application accepts only marks between 0 and 100. The grade is decided as follows: 90 and above = A+, 75 to 89 = A, 60 to 74 = B, 40 to 59 = C and below 40 = F. The marks are stored in an array in subject order, and file handling allows the record to be saved and loaded automatically.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
